From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 07:51:12 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: hold an event when the guest has no buffer
 posted

virtio_media_send_event() dropped an event when the guest had no event
buffer posted, and never sent it again. A comment in the event
code said an empty queue meant the guest had stopped consuming. It
does not: the driver handles events in a workqueue and reposts each
buffer after the event, so its 16 buffers can run out for a moment when
that work does not run soon enough.

A dropped event is enough to stop playback. The guest's v4l2_codec2
decoder completes its works in output order, and a work is done once
its input buffer is back and its output has arrived. When the
OUTPUT-done of one bitstream buffer is lost, that work never completes;
every later work waits behind it, holding its output buffer, until the
decoder has no output buffer left to queue, and decoding stalls too.
Measured on a Raspberry Pi 5 (2026-10-21), H.264 1080p60 played full
screen in a WebView with WebViewSurfaceControl, 10 minutes at a time:
playback stopped in 8 of 15 runs. In 3 of those runs a build logged
every drop: the only event dropped was the OUTPUT-done of one
bitstream buffer, in the one run that stopped, and the decoder's last
successful output-buffer dequeue came about 0.14 s later.

The device now holds an event the guest has no buffer for, in order,
and a new event waits behind the held ones, so an event can be late but
is never reordered. This layer's guest driver does not kick the event
queue when it reposts a buffer (its patch 0011), so the held events go
out with the next event the device sends, before the response to the
next command the guest sends, or from a timer: 1 ms after holding one;
each time the timer finds the guest has taken none the delay doubles,
up to 100 ms, and once it takes one it is back to 1 ms.
virtio_media_handle_event(), empty until now, sends them at once for a
driver that kicks when it reposts a buffer, as upstream's does.

A held event must not arrive after the command that took its buffer
back: STREAMOFF and REQBUFS(0) drop the held DQBUF events of that queue,
and CLOSE every held event of the session, before the held events that
remain are sent. Nothing is held before DRIVER_OK; an event then is for
a session of a driver that went away, and is sent if there is a buffer
and dropped if not, as before. A device reset, which the device now
handles, drops the held events and owed errors.

Held events live in a fixed array of 64, four times the buffers the
driver posts, so a guest that really stops consuming costs no more
memory and ten timer wake-ups a second. Past 64 an event is dropped
and reported (the first and every 100th), and its session, up to 8 of
them, is sent VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR once the held events have gone
out, so that it fails instead of waiting for the lost event; this
layer's guest keeps a session it got an error for until it is closed
(its patch 0015). At most every 30 s, when events waited since the last
report, the device reports how many did and how many were held at once.

With this change, the same test stopped in none of 6 runs, 3 with the
ioreq thread off and 3 with it on. At least 10 and 1 events waited
(the 30 s reports leave out the last seconds of a run), at most 8 at
once, and none was dropped.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on this layer's virtio-media device]
---
 hw/virtio/virtio-media.c         | 279 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 include/hw/virtio/virtio-media.h |  19 +++
 2 files changed, 283 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c b/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
index 40caf7455..4726b93c0 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio-media.c
@@ -504,16 +504,9 @@ bool virtio_media_sg_copy(VirtIOMedia *vm, VirtIOMediaPlaneSg *sg,
 /* events                                                                    */
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
 
-/*
- * Push one event onto the event queue.
- *
- * If the guest has no buffer posted the event is DROPPED, with a warning, and
- * not retried. Buffering it here instead would grow without bound while the
- * guest is wedged, and the driver keeps several buffers posted at all times
- * (virtio_media_send_event_buffer is called for each one at probe and again
- * after each event), so an empty queue means the guest has stopped consuming.
- */
-void virtio_media_send_event(VirtIOMedia *vm, const void *evt, size_t len)
+/* Put one event into a buffer the guest has posted; false if it has none. */
+static bool virtio_media_push_event(VirtIOMedia *vm, const void *evt,
+                                    size_t len)
 {
     VirtIODevice *vdev = VIRTIO_DEVICE(vm);
     VirtQueueElement *elem;
@@ -521,13 +514,226 @@ void virtio_media_send_event(VirtIOMedia *vm, const void *evt, size_t len)
 
     elem = virtqueue_pop(vm->event_vq, sizeof(VirtQueueElement));
     if (!elem) {
-        warn_report_once("virtio-media: event queue empty, dropping event");
-        return;
+        return false;
     }
     written = iov_from_buf(elem->in_sg, elem->in_num, 0, evt, len);
     virtqueue_push(vm->event_vq, elem, written);
     virtio_notify(vdev, vm->event_vq);
     g_free(elem);
+    return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Tell a session whose event had to be dropped (the held queue was full) that
+ * it lost one, once the guest can take it, so that it fails rather than waits
+ * forever for that event. This layer's guest keeps a session it got an error
+ * for until it is closed (aosp-kernel-patches .../virtio-media/0015).
+ */
+static void virtio_media_send_drop_errors(VirtIOMedia *vm)
+{
+    struct virtio_media_event_error evt = {
+        .hdr = { .event = VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR },
+        .err = EIO,
+    };
+
+    while (vm->evt_err_count) {
+        evt.hdr.session_id = vm->evt_err_sid[0];
+        if (!virtio_media_push_event(vm, &evt, sizeof(evt))) {
+            return;
+        }
+        vm->evt_err_count--;
+        memmove(vm->evt_err_sid, vm->evt_err_sid + 1,
+                vm->evt_err_count * sizeof(vm->evt_err_sid[0]));
+    }
+}
+
+/*
+ * Send the held events the guest has buffers for, oldest first, then any
+ * error owed for a dropped one, and retry the rest from a timer.
+ *
+ * WHEN. This layer's guest driver does not kick the event queue when it
+ * reposts a buffer (aosp-kernel-patches .../virtio-media/0011), only for the
+ * 16 it posts at probe, so the held events go out with the next event this
+ * device sends, before the response to the next command the guest sends, or
+ * from the timer: 1 ms after holding one; each time the timer finds that the
+ * guest has taken none, the delay doubles, up to 100 ms; once it takes one,
+ * back to 1 ms. A guest that has stopped consuming costs ten wake-ups a
+ * second, not a thousand.
+ */
+static void virtio_media_send_held_events(VirtIOMedia *vm, bool from_timer)
+{
+    VirtIOMediaHeldEvent *e;
+    uint32_t before = vm->evt_held_count + vm->evt_err_count;
+
+    while (vm->evt_held_count) {
+        e = &vm->evt_held[vm->evt_held_head];
+        if (!virtio_media_push_event(vm, e->buf, e->len)) {
+            break;
+        }
+        vm->evt_held_head = (vm->evt_held_head + 1) % VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX;
+        vm->evt_held_count--;
+    }
+    if (!vm->evt_held_count) {
+        virtio_media_send_drop_errors(vm);
+    }
+    if (!vm->evt_held_count && !vm->evt_err_count) {
+        timer_del(vm->evt_held_timer);
+        vm->evt_held_retry_us = 1000;
+        return;
+    }
+    if (vm->evt_held_count + vm->evt_err_count < before) {
+        vm->evt_held_retry_us = 1000;
+    } else if (from_timer) {
+        vm->evt_held_retry_us = MIN(vm->evt_held_retry_us * 2, 100000);
+    } else if (timer_pending(vm->evt_held_timer)) {
+        return;         /* no progress: leave the timer where it is */
+    }
+    timer_mod(vm->evt_held_timer,
+              qemu_clock_get_us(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME) + vm->evt_held_retry_us);
+}
+
+static void virtio_media_evt_held_timer_cb(void *opaque)
+{
+    virtio_media_send_held_events(opaque, true);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Drop the held events that must not reach the guest any more: after
+ * STREAMOFF or REQBUFS(0) on a queue (|type| != 0), the DQBUF events of that
+ * queue, whose buffers the guest owns again; after CLOSE (|type| == 0), every
+ * event of the session. The device already sends no event for such a buffer
+ * (see virtio_media_host_readable() and the decoder's STREAMOFF); this keeps
+ * one queued before the command from arriving after it.
+ */
+static void virtio_media_drop_held_events(VirtIOMedia *vm, uint32_t session_id,
+                                          uint32_t type)
+{
+    uint32_t i, kept = 0, n = vm->evt_held_count;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+        VirtIOMediaHeldEvent *e =
+            &vm->evt_held[(vm->evt_held_head + i) % VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX];
+        const struct virtio_media_event_header *h = (const void *)e->buf;
+        const struct virtio_media_event_dqbuf *d = (const void *)e->buf;
+
+        if (h->session_id == session_id &&
+            (!type || (h->event == VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_DQBUF &&
+                       d->buffer.type == type))) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (kept != i) {
+            vm->evt_held[(vm->evt_held_head + kept) %
+                         VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX] = *e;
+        }
+        kept++;
+    }
+    vm->evt_held_count = kept;
+    if (!type) {
+        for (i = 0; i < vm->evt_err_count; i++) {
+            if (vm->evt_err_sid[i] == session_id) {
+                vm->evt_err_count--;
+                memmove(vm->evt_err_sid + i, vm->evt_err_sid + i + 1,
+                        (vm->evt_err_count - i) * sizeof(vm->evt_err_sid[0]));
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    if (!vm->evt_held_count && !vm->evt_err_count) {
+        timer_del(vm->evt_held_timer);
+        vm->evt_held_retry_us = 1000;
+    }
+}
+
+/*
+ * Send one event, or hold it until the guest has a buffer for it.
+ *
+ * WHY HOLD. An event dropped here is never sent again, and the guest waits
+ * for it forever. A dropped OUTPUT-done is enough to stop playback: the
+ * guest's decoder completes its works in output order, and one whose input
+ * buffer never comes back blocks every later one, which keeps its output
+ * buffer; the decoder then has no output buffer left to queue, and decoding
+ * stalls too. Measured on RPi5 (2026-10-21), H.264 1080p60 in a WebView with
+ * WebViewSurfaceControl: in three runs with a build that logged every drop,
+ * the only event dropped was the OUTPUT-done of one bitstream buffer, in the
+ * one run that stopped, and the decoder's last successful output-buffer
+ * dequeue came about 0.14 s later. The guest had not stopped consuming: it
+ * handles events in a workqueue and reposts each buffer after the event, so
+ * its 16 buffers can run out for a moment when that work does not run soon
+ * enough.
+ *
+ * Held events keep their order, a new event waits behind them, and an event
+ * is only late, never reordered. They live in a fixed array of
+ * VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX, so a guest that really stops consuming costs no
+ * more memory; past that an event is dropped and reported, and its session
+ * (up to 8 of them) is sent VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR once the held events have
+ * gone out. Before the driver is ready (after a reset, until DRIVER_OK)
+ * nothing is held: an event then is for a session of the driver that went
+ * away, and is sent if there is a buffer and dropped if not, as before.
+ */
+void virtio_media_send_event(VirtIOMedia *vm, const void *evt, size_t len)
+{
+    VirtIODevice *vdev = VIRTIO_DEVICE(vm);
+    const struct virtio_media_event_header *h = evt;
+    VirtIOMediaHeldEvent *e;
+    uint32_t i;
+    int64_t now;
+
+    assert(len <= sizeof(e->buf));
+    if (vm->evt_held_count || vm->evt_err_count) {
+        virtio_media_send_held_events(vm, false);
+    }
+    if (!(vdev->status & VIRTIO_CONFIG_S_DRIVER_OK)) {
+        virtio_media_push_event(vm, evt, len);
+    } else if (vm->evt_held_count || vm->evt_err_count ||
+               !virtio_media_push_event(vm, evt, len)) {
+        if (vm->evt_held_count == VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX) {
+            vm->evt_held_dropped++;
+            for (i = 0; i < vm->evt_err_count; i++) {
+                if (vm->evt_err_sid[i] == h->session_id) {
+                    break;
+                }
+            }
+            if (i == vm->evt_err_count &&
+                vm->evt_err_count < ARRAY_SIZE(vm->evt_err_sid)) {
+                vm->evt_err_sid[vm->evt_err_count++] = h->session_id;
+            }
+            /* The first drop and every 100th after it. */
+            if (vm->evt_held_dropped % 100 == 1) {
+                warn_report("virtio-media: event dropped, the guest has taken "
+                            "none of %u held events (%" PRIu64 " dropped)",
+                            vm->evt_held_count, vm->evt_held_dropped);
+            }
+        } else {
+            e = &vm->evt_held[(vm->evt_held_head + vm->evt_held_count) %
+                              VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX];
+            memcpy(e->buf, evt, len);
+            e->len = len;
+            vm->evt_held_count++;
+            vm->evt_held_total++;
+            vm->evt_held_depth_max = MAX(vm->evt_held_depth_max,
+                                         vm->evt_held_count);
+            if (!timer_pending(vm->evt_held_timer)) {
+                vm->evt_held_retry_us = 1000;
+                timer_mod(vm->evt_held_timer,
+                          qemu_clock_get_us(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME) + 1000);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /* Every 30 s at most, and only when events waited since the last report. */
+    now = qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME);
+    if (vm->evt_held_total != vm->evt_held_reported &&
+        now - vm->evt_held_stats_ms >= 30000) {
+        info_report("virtio-media: %" PRIu64 " events waited for a guest event "
+                    "buffer (%" PRIu64 " in all), at most %u at once; %" PRIu64
+                    " dropped in all",
+                    vm->evt_held_total - vm->evt_held_reported,
+                    vm->evt_held_total, vm->evt_held_depth_max,
+                    vm->evt_held_dropped);
+        vm->evt_held_reported = vm->evt_held_total;
+        vm->evt_held_depth_max = 0;
+        vm->evt_held_stats_ms = now;
+    }
 }
 
 void virtio_media_send_error_event(VirtIOMediaSession *s, int err)
@@ -2100,6 +2306,7 @@ static int virtio_media_do_close(VirtIOMedia *vm, uint32_t session_id)
     if (!s) {
         return EINVAL;
     }
+    virtio_media_drop_held_events(vm, session_id, 0);
     QLIST_REMOVE(s, next);
     virtio_media_session_free(s);
     return 0;
@@ -3018,6 +3225,16 @@ static void virtio_media_command_bh(void *opaque)
                 status = virtio_media_passthrough(s, desc->code, payload,
                                                   work_len);
             }
+            if (!status && desc->code == VIDIOC_STREAMOFF &&
+                payload_len >= sizeof(uint32_t)) {
+                virtio_media_drop_held_events(vm, s->id,
+                                              *(uint32_t *)payload);
+            } else if (!status && desc->code == VIDIOC_REQBUFS &&
+                       payload_len >= sizeof(struct v4l2_requestbuffers) &&
+                       !((struct v4l2_requestbuffers *)payload)->count) {
+                virtio_media_drop_held_events(
+                    vm, s->id, ((struct v4l2_requestbuffers *)payload)->type);
+            }
 
             /*
              * The response payload exists only for _IOR / _IOWR. Its size comes
@@ -3067,6 +3284,15 @@ static void virtio_media_command_bh(void *opaque)
         if (resp_len) {
             iov_from_buf(elem->in_sg, elem->in_num, 0, resp, resp_len);
         }
+        /*
+         * The guest is at work: send what it has buffers for, after this
+         * command has dropped the events it took back (STREAMOFF,
+         * REQBUFS(0), CLOSE) and before its response, so that an event the
+         * guest has a buffer for still comes before the response.
+         */
+        if (vm->evt_held_count || vm->evt_err_count) {
+            virtio_media_send_held_events(vm, false);
+        }
         virtqueue_push(vq, elem, resp_len);
         g_free(elem);
     }
@@ -3090,12 +3316,13 @@ static void virtio_media_handle_command(VirtIODevice *vdev, VirtQueue *vq)
 }
 
 /*
- * The event queue only ever carries device-to-driver traffic. The driver posts
- * empty buffers on it and this handler does nothing with the kick: buffers are
- * consumed by virtio_media_send_event() when there is something to report.
+ * The event queue only ever carries device-to-driver traffic. A driver that
+ * kicks it when it reposts a buffer, as upstream's does, gets the held events
+ * at once. This layer's driver kicks only at probe, when nothing is held.
  */
 static void virtio_media_handle_event(VirtIODevice *vdev, VirtQueue *vq)
 {
+    virtio_media_send_held_events(VIRTIO_MEDIA(vdev), false);
 }
 
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@@ -3219,6 +3446,10 @@ init_common:
     vm->command_vq = virtio_add_queue(vdev, 64, virtio_media_handle_command);
     vm->event_vq = virtio_add_queue(vdev, 64, virtio_media_handle_event);
     vm->command_bh = virtio_bh_new_guarded(dev, virtio_media_command_bh, vm);
+    vm->evt_held_timer = timer_new_us(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME,
+                                      virtio_media_evt_held_timer_cb, vm);
+    vm->evt_held_retry_us = 1000;
+    vm->evt_held_stats_ms = qemu_clock_get_ms(QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME);
     virtio_media_gpu_set_fence_fd(vm->gpu_fence_fd);
 
     /*
@@ -3238,6 +3469,22 @@ init_common:
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * A device reset drops the held events and owed errors: the driver they were
+ * for is gone.
+ */
+static void virtio_media_reset(VirtIODevice *vdev)
+{
+    VirtIOMedia *vm = VIRTIO_MEDIA(vdev);
+
+    vm->evt_held_head = 0;
+    vm->evt_held_count = 0;
+    vm->evt_err_count = 0;
+    if (vm->evt_held_timer) {
+        timer_del(vm->evt_held_timer);
+    }
+}
+
 static void virtio_media_unrealize(DeviceState *dev)
 {
     VirtIODevice *vdev = VIRTIO_DEVICE(dev);
@@ -3249,6 +3496,7 @@ static void virtio_media_unrealize(DeviceState *dev)
         virtio_media_session_free(s);
     }
     g_clear_pointer(&vm->command_bh, qemu_bh_delete);
+    g_clear_pointer(&vm->evt_held_timer, timer_free);
     g_free(vm->cmd_buf);
     vm->cmd_buf = NULL;
     virtio_delete_queue(vm->command_vq);
@@ -3295,6 +3543,7 @@ static void virtio_media_class_init(ObjectClass *klass, const void *data)
     set_bit(DEVICE_CATEGORY_MISC, dc->categories);
     vdc->realize = virtio_media_realize;
     vdc->unrealize = virtio_media_unrealize;
+    vdc->reset = virtio_media_reset;
     vdc->get_config = virtio_media_get_config;
     vdc->get_features = virtio_media_get_features;
 }
diff --git a/include/hw/virtio/virtio-media.h b/include/hw/virtio/virtio-media.h
index c4a78ce14..e7ef9ac3a 100644
--- a/include/hw/virtio/virtio-media.h
+++ b/include/hw/virtio/virtio-media.h
@@ -142,6 +142,16 @@ struct virtio_media_event_event {
  */
 #define VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVENT_MAX_SIZE sizeof(struct virtio_media_event_dqbuf)
 
+/*
+ * An event the guest had no buffer for yet; see virtio_media_send_event().
+ * 64 is four times the buffers the guest driver posts.
+ */
+#define VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX 64
+typedef struct VirtIOMediaHeldEvent {
+    uint8_t buf[VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVENT_MAX_SIZE];
+    uint32_t len;
+} VirtIOMediaHeldEvent;
+
 /*
  * Enough for any single V4L2 ioctl payload plus the arrays that follow it: a
  * v4l2_buffer with VIDEO_MAX_PLANES planes and one SG list per plane. A 4 KiB
@@ -481,6 +491,15 @@ struct VirtIOMedia {
      * allocation out of the path.
      */
     uint8_t *cmd_buf;
+    VirtIOMediaHeldEvent evt_held[VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_HELD_MAX];
+    uint32_t evt_held_head, evt_held_count, evt_held_depth_max;
+    QEMUTimer *evt_held_timer;
+    int64_t evt_held_retry_us;
+    uint64_t evt_held_total, evt_held_reported, evt_held_dropped;
+    int64_t evt_held_stats_ms;
+    /* sessions owed an error for an event dropped with the held queue full */
+    uint32_t evt_err_sid[8];
+    uint32_t evt_err_count;
 };
 
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
